'''4.''' Having glowing or fiery passions or affections: as applied to the emotions themselves, showing vehemence; passionate; affectionate; zealous: as, ''ardent'' love or vows; ''ardent'' zeal.
{{smaller block|<poem>Not ''ardent'' lovers robb’d of all their bliss,
Not ancient ladies when refused a kiss, . . .
E’er felt such rage, resentment, and despair
As thou, sad virgin! for thy ravish’d hair.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', R. of the L., iv. 5.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>His form accorded with a mind
Lively and ''ardent'', frank and kind.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of the L., ii. 25.}}}}
'''5†.''' ''Naut.'', having a tendency to gripe or come quickly to the wind: said of certain ships.—{{smaller|'''Ardent spirits''', distilled alcoholic liquors, as brandy, whisky, gin, rum, etc. They are all produced by the distillation of fermented vegetable juices containing sugar.}} ='''Syn. 3''' and '''4.''' {{smaller|Fiery, intense, eager, keen, fervid, fervent, impassioned, glowing.}}

'''ardently''' (är′dẹnt-li), ''adv.'' [ME. ''ardontliche''; < ''ardent'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In an ardent manner; with warmth; affectionately; passionately.

'''ardentness''' (är′dẹnt-nes), ''n.'' [< ME. ''ardentnesse.''] The state or quality of being ardent; ardency.

'''Ardeoideæ''' (är-dē-oi′dẹ̄-ē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Ardea'' + ''-oideæ.''] A superfamily group of herodionine birds.

'''Ardeola''' (är-dē′ọ̄-lä̤), ''n.'' [L., a little heron, dim. of ''ardea'', a heron.] A genus of small and somewhat rail-like herons, the squacco herons, of the subfamily ''Ardeinæ.'' {{smaller|The type is ''A. comata'' or ''A. ralloides'', of Europe; but there are several other species. ''J. F. Boie'', 1822.}}

